  "action": "Researched the original jarrodwatts/jev-trader repo (librarian lane failed with upstream 'Insufficient account funds', so cloned the repo directly via git and read it). KEY FINDING: the original is NOT profitable and is explicitly NOT trying to be. SPEC.md footer: 'experimental demo, tiny bankroll, not financial advice, the model is not trying to be profitable.' Its success criterion is virality of a 20s clip, and its punchline is 'The AI costs less than the gas.' It is NOT a Binance bot: it trades MON-USDC on Kuru, an on-chain order book on Monad L1 (chainId 143), one decision per ~300ms Monad block, posting a post-only limit order 1 tick inside the touch and cancelling the previous one (README: 'so the bot earns the spread instead of paying it'; latest commit message: 'Post-only limit orders every block: earn the spread instead of paying it'). COST MODEL: there is NO trading fee at all - the only cost is Monad GAS (totals.gasMon/gasUsd; pnlUsd = realizedUsd + unrealized - gasUsd; gas charged on the gas LIMIT). Spreads are wide (~7.07 bps on MON-USDC) vs BTCUSDT spot (~0.0012 bps). Also found the original's model prompt text is internally inconsistent with its implementation (the question says the order is an 'immediate-or-cancel market order' that 'crosses the spread' while the code is post-only maker that EARNS the spread) - my port inherited this inconsistency. No recorded real P&L session exists in the repo (no data/ dir); the deployed demo is dry-run + mock model. Recommended implementation is a venue-faithful redesign (gas-style per-order cost + a market whose spread exceeds cost) rather than the CEX-taker-fee model my port used."
